==================================================
STEP 4 — VALIDATE METRIC DEFINITIONS
==================================================

Do not invent business definitions.

Flag any metric whose definition is ambiguous.

Specifically analyze:

1. “Questions Tested”

Determine whether this should mean:
- all chats/questions submitted by the tester
or
- only questions explicitly submitted as validation/test questions
or
- only questions for which feedback was submitted

Do not choose without evidence.

2. “Agent Coverage”

Determine whether this means:
- distinct agents used / all available agents
or
- distinct required validation agents used / required agents
or
- some predefined validation threshold

3. “Feedback Coverage”

Preferred formula if supported:

questions with feedback / total questions tested

But validate this against the existing data model.

4. “Positive Feedback Rate”

Preferred formula:

positive feedback / total submitted feedback

Do NOT calculate:

positive feedback / total questions

unless that is the existing business definition.

5. “Issues Found”

Determine whether a negative feedback record automatically constitutes an issue.

Check whether the application already has:
- issue_status
- resolution_status
- review_status
- ticket/reference
- feedback category

If not, flag this metric as NOT FOUND rather than inventing it.

6. “Needs Review”

Check whether this is a real existing status.
If it does not exist, clearly state that.

7. “Regression Status”

Determine whether chat/question records are currently linked to the regression dataset/results.

Identify:
- regression question ID
- included/not included
- pass/fail
- last run
- regression version

If the link does not exist, flag the gap.

